          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now you have become familiar with many properties"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.102,
            0.456,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "of materials. However, all materials do not possess all these properties."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.178,
            0.38,
            0.202
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.4 What is Matter?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.213,
            0.453,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mass and volume are the two properties that are possessed by all materials. Can we give a general name to anything that possesses these two properties?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.138,
            0.699,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are th"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ere",
              "styles": [
                "subscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " an properties which can be shown by all materials? If yes. what are those?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.316,
            0.745,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Anything that occupies space and has mass is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "matter",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The mass gives the quantity of matter, and the units to measure it are gram (g) and kilogram (kg). The space occupied by matter is its volume. The units to measure the volume are litre (L) and millilitre (mL)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.47,
            0.718,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Kilogram is the unit of mass in the International System of Units (SI). Kilogram is abbreviated in lower case as kg. There is no space between ‘k’ and ‘g’ in kg, and no full stop after the symbol, except at the end of a sentence. While writing the mass, always leave a space between the number (numerical value) and the unit. For example, if we have mass of 7 kilograms, it would be written as 7 kg and not as 7 kgs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.633,
            0.718,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, litre is abbreviated as capital L and millilitre as mL. There is no space between ‘m’ and ‘L’ in mL. For example, if you have 500 millilitres of water, it would be written as 500 mL, m will be in lower case and L will be in upper case. The SI unit for volume is cubic metre, abbreviated as m"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The abbreviation is written with a superscript 3 to denote cubic metre. For example, if you have volume of 2 cubic metres, it would be written o y D ou as 2 m"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Always leave a space between number know? knov (numerical value) and the unit. 1 m"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " =1000 L."
            }
          ]
        },
